"Summary**\n\n**a. Verdict:**  \nI have rigorously proved that for \\(n=3\\) it is **impossible** to have a configuration of three distinct lines in which exactly two of the lines are sunny. Hence the integer \\(k=2\\) does **not** belong to the set of admissible values.\n\n**b. Method Sketch:**  \n\n1. **List the required points.**  \n   For \\(n=3\\) the condition \\(a+b\\le n+1=4\\) yields the six lattice points  \n\n   \\[\n   P_{1}=(1,1),\\;P_{2}=(1,2),\\;P_{3}=(1,3),\\;P_{4}=(2,1),\\;P_{5}=(2,2),\\;P_{6}=(3,1).\n   \\]\n\n2. **Identify all possible sunny lines.**  \n   A sunny line must have slope different from \\(0\\) (horizontal), \\(\\infty\\) (vertical) and \\(-1\\) (the slope of \\(x+y=0\\)).  \n   Computing the slopes of all \\(\\binom{6}{2}=15\\) pairs shows that the only pairs whose connecting line has a sunny slope are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{P_{2},P_{6}\\}\\quad\\text{with slope }-\\tfrac12,\n   \\qquad\n   \\{P_{3},P_{4}\\}\\quad\\text{with slope }-2 .\n   \\]\n\n   Consequently **every sunny line can contain at most two of the six points**, and the only sunny lines that contain two points are the two lines above.\n\n3. **Assume a configuration with exactly two sunny lines exists.**  \n   Since each sunny line can cover at most two points, the two sunny lines together can cover at most four points.  \n   To cover all six points with three lines, the remaining (non\u2011sunny) line must contain the two uncovered points.\n\n4. **Show the uncovered points cannot lie on a non\u2011sunny line.**  \n   The only way for the two sunny lines to cover four points is to use **both** of the sunny lines found in step\u202f2, because any other sunny line would contain at most one point.  \n   Thus the four points covered are  \n\n   \\[\n   \\{P_{2},P_{6}\\}\\cup\\{P_{3},P_{4}\\}\n   =\\{(1,2),(3,1),(1,3),(2,1)\\}.\n   \\]\n\n   The two points left uncovered are \\(P_{1}=(1,1)\\) and \\(P_{5}=(2,2)\\).  \n   The line through these two points has slope  \n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{2-1}{2-1}=1,\n   \\]\n\n   which is **neither** \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) nor \\(-1\\); hence it is a **sunny** line, contradicting the assumption that only two lines are sunny.\n\n5. **Conclusion.**  \n   No arrangement of three distinct lines can satisfy the covering condition while having exactly two sunny lines. Therefore \\(k=2\\) is impossible for \\(n=3\\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\text{For }n=3\\text{, the value }k=2\\text{ cannot be attained.}}\n\\]"
