 level of each residue class modulo \\(|p+1|\\).  Since the levels \\(S_{2},\\dots ,S_{m+1}\\) are \\(m\\) distinct residues, any family of sunny lines covering all points of \\(T_{m}\\) must contain at least one line for each of the \\(m\\) residues; hence at least \\(m\\) lines are necessary.\n\nTo show that \\(m\\) lines are sufficient, for each \\(i\\in\\{0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y = (i+1)\\,x + i .\n\\tag{3}\n\\]\n\nBecause \\(i+1\\neq 0,-1\\), each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nFor a given level \\(S_{s}\\) (\\(2\\le s\\le m+1\\)) set  \n\n\\[\ni\\equiv s-2\\pmod{i+2}.\n\\]\n\nA direct computation using (1) shows that the unique integer \\(i\\) with \\(0\\le i\\le m-1\\) satisfying this congruence yields a point of \\(S_{s}\\) lying on \\(L_{i}\\); explicitly the point is  \n\n\\[\n\\bigl(a,b\\bigr)=\\Bigl(\\frac{s-i}{i+2},\\;\\frac{(i+1)s+i}{i+2}\\Bigr),\n\\]\n\nand one checks that \\(1\\le a,b\\le s-1\\).  Hence each level \\(S_{s}\\) contributes exactly one point to the family \\(\\{L_{0},\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\), and the union of these \\(m\\) lines is precisely \\(T_{m}\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for an arbitrary number \\(k\\) of sunny lines\n\nFix \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny anti‑diagonal lines.**  \n   For every integer  \n\n   \\[\n   s\\in\\{k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\}\n   \\]\n\n   take the line  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{s}:\\; x+y=s .\n   \\tag{4}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_{s}\\) is parallel to the forbidden line \\(x+y=0\\) and therefore non‑sunny.  Moreover \\(D_{s}\\) contains **all** points of the level \\(S_{s}\\).\n\n   The number of such lines is \\((n+1)-(k+2)+1 = n-k\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines for the remaining points.**  \n   The points that are not yet covered are exactly  \n\n   \\[\n   \\bigcup_{s=2}^{k+1} S_{s}=T_{k}.\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; an explicit choice is the family \\(\\{L_{0},L_{1},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) defined in (3) with \\(m=k\\).\n\n3. **Verification of the required properties.**  \n\n   *All points are covered.*  \n   The anti‑diagonal lines (4) cover every point whose sum belongs to \\(\\{k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\}\\); the sunny lines cover all points whose sum belongs to \\(\\{2,\\dots ,k+1\\}\\).  Hence every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies on at least one of the \\(n\\) lines.\n\n   *Exactly \\(k\\) sunny lines.*  \n   The family \\(\\{L_{0},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) consists of sunny lines, while the \\(n-k\\) lines \\(D_{s}\\) are non‑sunny.  No line appears twice because the slopes of the \\(L_{i}\\) are \\(i+1\\neq -1,0\\) and the slopes of the \\(D_{s}\\) are \\(-1\\).  Hence the total number of distinct lines is \\(k+(n-k)=n\\), with precisely \\(k\\) sunny ones.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a configuration of \\(n\\) distinct lines satisfying the two conditions of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Impossibility of other values of \\(k\\)\n\nThe definition of a sunny line excludes the three directions listed in the statement; consequently a line is either sunny or belongs to the three families described above.  Since we have exactly \\(n\\) lines, the number of sunny lines cannot exceed \\(n\\) and cannot be negative.  Therefore the only possible values of \\(k\\) are the integers between \\(0\\) and \\(n\\), all of which have been shown to be attainable.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n**Conclusion**  \n\nFor every integer \\(n\\ge 3\\) the set of admissible numbers of sunny lines is  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}} .\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors o