            "content": "\n======================================================================\n### Problem ###\n\n*** Problem Statement ***\n\nA line in the plane is called *sunny* if it is not parallel to any of the $x$-axis, the $y$-axis, and the line $x+y=0$.\n\nLet $n\\ge3$ be a given integer. Determine all nonnegative integers $k$ such that there exist $n$ distinct lines in the plane satisfying both the following:\n*   for all positive integers $a$ and $b$ with $a+b\\le n+1$, the point $(a,b)$ is on at least one of the lines; and\n*   exactly $k$ of the lines are sunny.\n\n======================================================================\n### Solution ###\n\n**\n\nLet  \n\n\\[\n\\mathcal P_{n}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathbb Z^{2}\\mid a\\ge 1,\\;b\\ge 1,\\;a+b\\le n+1\\},\n\\qquad n\\ge 3 .\n\\]\n\nFor each integer \\(s\\) with \\(2\\le s\\le n+1\\) put  \n\n\\[\nS_{s}= \\{(a,b)\\in\\mathcal P_{n}\\mid a+b=s\\},\n\\qquad |S_{s}|=s-1 .\n\\]\n\nA line is **sunny** iff its slope is different from \\(0\\), \\(\\infty\\) and \\(-1\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Lemma 1  \n\nFor any integer \\(m\\ge 1\\) the set  \n\n\\[\nT_{m}= \\bigcup_{s=2}^{m+1}S_{s}\n\\]\n\ncan be covered by exactly \\(m\\) distinct sunny lines, and it cannot be covered by fewer than \\(m\\) sunny lines.\n\n*Proof.*  \nThe level \\(S_{m+1}\\) contains \\(m\\) points.  A sunny line is never parallel to the anti‑diagonal, therefore it meets each level in at most one point.  Hence at least \\(m\\) sunny lines are necessary.\n\nFor \\(i=0,1,\\dots ,m-1\\) define  \n\n\\[\nL_{i}:\\; y=(i+1)x+i .\n\\tag{1}\n\\]\n\nBecause the slope \\(i+1\\) is neither \\(0\\), \\(-1\\) nor \\(\\infty\\), each \\(L_{i}\\) is sunny.  \nIntersecting \\(L_{i}\\) with the line \\(x+y=s\\) gives  \n\n\\[\nx=\\frac{s-i}{i+2},\\qquad y=\\frac{(i+1)s+i}{i+2}.\n\\tag{2}\n\\]\n\nThus \\(L_{i}\\) meets the level \\(S_{s}\\) **iff** \\(s\\equiv i\\pmod{i+2}\\); in that case the intersection point is the integer pair displayed in (2), and one checks that its coordinates satisfy \\(1\\le x,y\\le s-1\\).  \n\nFor every \\(s\\in\\{2,\\dots ,m+1\\}\\) there is a unique \\(i\\in\\{0,\\dots ,m-1\\}\\) with \\(s\\equiv i\\pmod{i+2}\\); consequently exactly one of the lines \\(L_{i}\\) contains a point of \\(S_{s}\\).  Hence the family \\(\\{L_{0},\\dots ,L_{m-1}\\}\\) covers every level \\(S_{s}\\) and therefore the whole set \\(T_{m}\\).  ∎\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Construction for a prescribed number \\(k\\) of sunny lines  \n\nLet \\(k\\) be an integer with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\).\n\n1. **Non‑sunny anti‑diagonal lines.**  \n   Use the \\(n-k\\) lines  \n\n   \\[\n   D_{t}:\\;x+y=t\\qquad\\bigl(t=k+2,\\;k+3,\\dots ,n+1\\bigr) .\n   \\tag{3}\n   \\]\n\n   Each \\(D_{t}\\) is non‑sunny and contains **all** points of the level \\(S_{t}\\).\n\n2. **Sunny lines for the remaining levels.**  \n   The union of the still uncovered levels is  \n\n   \\[\n   T_{k}= \\bigcup_{s=2}^{k+1}S_{s}.\n   \\]\n\n   By Lemma 1, \\(T_{k}\\) can be covered by exactly \\(k\\) distinct sunny lines; an explicit choice is the family \\(\\{L_{0},\\dots ,L_{k-1}\\}\\) given by (1) with \\(m=k\\).\n\n3. **Verification.**  \n   The lines in (3) are all parallel to the anti‑diagonal, whereas each line \\(L_{i}\\) has slope \\(i+1\\neq -1\\); consequently the whole collection consists of \\(n\\) distinct lines.  By construction every point of \\(\\mathcal P_{n}\\) lies either on a line \\(D_{t}\\) (if its sum equals one of the integers \\(k+2,\\dots ,n+1\\)) or on one of the sunny lines \\(L_{i}\\) (if its sum is at most \\(k+1\\)).  Exactly the \\(k\\) lines \\(L_{i}\\) are sunny.\n\nThus for **every** integer \\(k\\) with \\(0\\le k\\le n\\) we have exhibited a configuration satisfying the two requirements of the problem.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### The exceptional case \\(n=3,\\;k=2\\)\n\nAssume that \\(n=3\\) and that three distinct lines contain all six points of \\(\\mathcal P_{3}\\) with exactly two of them sunny.\n\nBecause a sunny line meets each level in at most one point, the three points of the highest level  \n\n\\[\nS_{4}= \\{(1,3),(2,2),(3,1)\\}\n\\]\n\nmust be covered by the single non‑sunny line.  The only non‑sunny line that can contain all three points is the anti‑diagonal \\(x+y=4\\).\n\nAfter removing those three points we are left with the four points  \n\n\\[\n(1,1),\\;(1,2),\\;(2,1),\\;(2,2).\n\\]\n\nThe two sunny lines can each contain at most one of the points \\((1,2)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) (they lie on the forbidden line of slope \\(-1\\)).  Hence one of the remaining points, say \\((1,2)\\), must be covered together with \\((1,1)\\).  Any line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((1,2)\\) is vertical, i.e. non‑sunny; similarly a line through \\((1,1)\\) and \\((2,1)\\) is horizontal, also non‑sunny.  This contradicts the assumption that the two remaining lines are sunny.  Therefore no configuration with \\(k=2\\) exists when \\(n=3\\).\n\nFor \\(n=3\\) the constructions of the previous section give configurations for \\(k=0,1,3\\); together with the impossibility proved above we obtain the exact set \\(\\{0,1,3\\}\\).\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------\n### Final answer  \n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\n\\begin{cases}\n\\{0,1,3\\}, & n=3,\\\\[2mm]\n\\{0,1,2,\\dots ,n\\}, & n\\ge 4 .\n\\end{cases}}\n\\]\n\n\n### Verification Task Reminder ###\n\nYour task is to act as an IMO grader. Now, generate the **summary** and the **step-by-step verification log** for the solution above. In your log, justify each correct step and explain in detail any errors or justification gaps you find, as specified in the instructions above.\n\n"
